{
  "technical_and_design_decisions": [
    "Implement acceptance tests as black-box, end-to-end tests: each app module is packaged and launched as a standalone Spring Boot application and exercised through its real input/output bindings.",
    "Use real middleware (RabbitMQ or Kafka) running in Docker containers during tests so that acceptance tests validate realistic stream behavior rather than an in-memory test binder.",
    "Use containerized or embedded test doubles for external systems: GreenMail (embedded mail server) for the Mail source, Apache MINA SSHD (embedded SFTP server) for the SFTP source, WireMock/MockWebServer for the http-client processor, and a containerized Redis/JDBC store for the aggregator's state.",
    "Create a shared acceptance-test harness module providing common utilities: app launcher with property injection, readiness/health polling, message capture from binder destinations, assertion helpers, and log capture for diagnostics.",
    "Organize tests with JUnit 5, integrated into the Maven build under a dedicated acceptance-test profile/stage so they execute on every CI build and produce standard test reports (surefire/failsafe XML and HTML).",
    "Follow the Spring Framework 6 / Spring Boot 3 baseline with Java 17 as required by the specification.",
    "Design tests to be idempotent and isolated (unique queues/topics and ports per test run, random port allocation) to avoid flaky interactions and allow parallel CI execution.",
    "Keep production module code unchanged; only test-scope code, fixtures, and build configuration are added."
  ],
  "assumptions": [
    "All eight modules (Mail, reactor-ip, reactor-syslog, SFTP sources; aggregator, analytic-pmml, bridge, http-client processors) already exist, build successfully, and are functionally complete; the increment covers acceptance test coverage only.",
    "A CI system (e.g., Jenkins or GitHub Actions) already exists and its build agents support running Docker containers for test fixtures.",
    "Existing unit/integration tests are present in the modules; the acceptance tests complement rather than replace them.",
    "Sample test artifacts (e.g., a small valid PMML model for analytic-pmml, sample syslog messages, sample files for SFTP) can be created by the developer without external dependencies.",
    "One developer performs all work, including test design, implementation, CI wiring, and documentation; a workday equals 8 hours of focused effort.",
    "Defects discovered in modules during testing are reported but their functional fixes are out of scope, except for trivial configuration/test-enabling adjustments included in the stabilization activity.",
    "Estimates exclude elapsed-time waiting (e.g., CI queue time, reviewer availability); only actual development effort is counted."
  ],
  "activities": [
    {
      "activity_id": "A1",
      "activity_name": "Requirements analysis and existing infrastructure assessment",
      "activity_description": "Review all eight modules (configuration properties, expected behavior, existing unit/integration tests), review the current CI pipeline and build structure, and define the acceptance test scope, scenarios, and coverage targets per module.",
      "most_likely_effort": 2
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A2",
      "activity_name": "Acceptance test harness design and implementation",
      "activity_description": "Design and implement the shared test framework: app launcher utilities, Docker container management for middleware and fixtures, readiness/health polling, message capture/assertion helpers, and base test classes reused by all module test suites.",
      "most_likely_effort": 4
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A3",
      "activity_name": "Mail source acceptance tests (U1)",
      "activity_description": "Set up an embedded mail server fixture (GreenMail), implement acceptance tests covering mail receipt triggering, payload and header mapping, content-type handling, and attachment scenarios; wire the suite into the CI test stage.",
      "most_likely_effort": 4
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A4",
      "activity_name": "reactor-ip source acceptance tests (U2)",
      "activity_description": "Implement acceptance tests for TCP and UDP ingestion, including socket fixtures, length-field framing/decoder options, message ordering, and error handling for malformed input; wire the suite into the CI test stage.",
      "most_likely_effort": 3
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A5",
      "activity_name": "reactor-syslog source acceptance tests (U3)",
      "activity_description": "Implement acceptance tests sending RFC3164/RFC5424 syslog messages over UDP and TCP, verifying parsed payload/fields, message boundaries, and transport configuration options; wire the suite into the CI test stage.",
      "most_likely_effort": 3
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A6",
      "activity_name": "SFTP source acceptance tests (U4)",
      "activity_description": "Set up an embedded SFTP server fixture (Apache MINA SSHD) with user authentication, and implement acceptance tests for file detection/polling, single and multiple file transfer, file-content verification, and remote/local directory handling; wire the suite into the CI test stage.",
      "most_likely_effort": 4
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A7",
      "activity_name": "aggregator processor acceptance tests (U5)",
      "activity_description": "Implement acceptance tests for stateful aggregation using a containerized state store (Redis/JDBC): correlated message groups, aggregation and release strategies, store persistence across restarts, and timeout/size-based release scenarios; wire the suite into the CI test stage.",
      "most_likely_effort": 5
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A8",
      "activity_name": "analytic-pmml processor acceptance tests (U6)",
      "activity_description": "Create sample PMML models and input data, and implement acceptance tests verifying model loading from configurable locations, tuple/JSON input evaluation, output field extraction, and error behavior for invalid models/inputs; wire the suite into the CI test stage.",
      "most_likely_effort": 4
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A9",
      "activity_name": "bridge processor acceptance tests (U7)",
      "activity_description": "Implement acceptance tests verifying pass-through bridging of messages from an input destination to an output destination, header preservation, and binder configuration; wire the suite into the CI test stage.",
      "most_likely_effort": 2
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A10",
      "activity_name": "http-client processor acceptance tests (U8)",
      "activity_description": "Implement acceptance tests using a mock HTTP server (WireMock/MockWebServer) covering request methods and headers, expression-driven URL/body mapping, response-to-message mapping, and error/status handling; wire the suite into the CI test stage.",
      "most_likely_effort": 3
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A11",
      "activity_name": "CI pipeline integration",
      "activity_description": "Integrate all acceptance test suites into the CI build as a dedicated, build-gating stage: configure the acceptance-test Maven profile, Docker availability on agents, parallel execution where safe, and publication of test results and failure artifacts.",
      "most_likely_effort": 3
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A12",
      "activity_name": "Stabilization and review rework",
      "activity_description": "Run repeated CI executions to detect and fix flaky tests (timing, port/queue collisions, container startup races), apply code review feedback, and ensure the full suite passes reliably on every build.",
      "most_likely_effort": 4
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A13",
      "activity_name": "Documentation",
      "activity_description": "Document the acceptance test architecture, how to run the suites locally and in CI, fixture prerequisites, and per-module coverage notes; update module READMEs where applicable.",
      "most_likely_effort": 2
    }
  ],
  "reported_total": {
    "most_likely_effort": 43
  }
}
```

